In news:<1136265657.503268.233100@o13g2000cwo.googlegroups.com>,  
<ferg200@hotmail.com> says...
> Power is not a problem, the ARM chipset is not a problem, is the OS a
> problem.

I am not a good person to answer that. But to try anyway, I would say 
that 1) there are many phones available for which processing and memory 
requirements are no problems, 2) some of these have operating systems 
which make porting Nethack to them not-so-difficult, 3) the main 
problems that still exist then are a) display capabilities, b) 
control/input capabilities, and c) someone must be interested and 
knowledgeable enough to do the work.
 
> If I am reading right, the reason I can't play nethack on my phone
> isn't because it can't be done, it's because it hasn't been done.
> Right?

Depending on the phone. For some phones it might require such-enormous-
work-that-noone-will-ever-do-it, for some it might require just a lot of 
work, for some it will only require a little work, and for some it's 
already done.

If you have a Windows CE based phone you might be lucky and it's already 
done for you. You might not like the limitations imposed by the display 
and control capabilities dictated by the device though.

I don't know much about phones able to run Linux, but depending on how 
limited these Linux versions are it might not be a very difficult task 
to make a useful Nethack port for one. This will in most cases require 
some non-trivial work on adapting Nethack's display and controls to the 
device in point.

Even if you have a phone which has a good telnet app (and can display a 
telnet session in a nethack-useful way) and you don't mind playing over 
telnet, you will need a practical way to control Nethack. That could be 
if your phone has a usable keyboard (built-in or accessory, hardware or 
software) or supports some other special method of handling the controls 
that does not mean you have to do 10 keypresses every time you want to 
look in your sack.

The main limitations today (asuming that the phone has adequately 
powerful hardware and is running an operating system that does not 
present too much problems porting to) are then 1) controls 2) display 3) 
someone-wanting-to-do-the-work-and-able-to-do-it. Phones that have the 
computing power, the needed RAM, the non-volatile memory for saves are 
plenty these days. The display capabilities are becoming less and less 
of a problem.

> Kristoffer, could you tell us what phone you are using?

I'm using the original Orange SPV, an Orange branded phone based on the 
Canary hardware platform produced by HTC. Slightly revised versions of 
this were also available with the QTEK brand. See this page for 
information about different Smartphone platforms produced by HTC, and 
some of their different branded/market names: 
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Tech_Computer_Corporation
Some of these phones, like mine, need to be 'unlocked' to allow non-
signed software to run.

The official binary is made for the Microsoft Smartphone 2002 operating 
system, something that I don't think any new phone is produced with or 
even able to run (without heavy hacking).

On http://nethack.org/v343/ports/download-wince.html#smp you also see 
some other phone model suggestions:
* "T-Mobile model SDA (aka HTC Thyphoon) under 2003 SE" - This is the 
same platform as Orange SPV C500, Qtek 8010, i-mate SP3 and AudioVox SMT 
5600. "2003 SE" refers to "Windows Mobile 2003 for Smartphone Second 
Edition"
* Motorola Mpx220 - AFAIK this phone is also using the 2003 SE system.

Microsoft Smartphone 2002 is a branch off Windows CE v3.0, and Windows 
Mobile 2003 (including Second Edition) is a branch off Windows CE v4.2. 
As Windows CE is now up to v5.0, and Windows Mobile is at version 
"2005" based off this, with CE v6.0 coming this year, it might seem that 
the Windows CE ports of Nethack is lagging behind a bit. But I have not 
tried the newer CE/Mobile versions - perhaps the old binaries work fine, 
and there is no need for updates, someone else will have to find this 
out though.

/Kristoffer

